1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,460
Twenty-four thousand dollars.

2
00:00:02,380 --> 00:00:06,420
That's what a retired couple can hand the
I R S by leaving room empty…

3
00:00:06,940 --> 00:00:11,190
room the tax code gave them for twenty
twenty-seven, and takes back when Social

4
00:00:11,240 --> 00:00:12,260
Security starts.

5
00:00:13,300 --> 00:00:14,860
This is Run the Number.

6
00:00:15,440 --> 00:00:18,580
By the end you'll know three figures for
twenty twenty-seven.

7
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:23,950
The floor: the amount a retired couple can
take from an I R A and pay nothing

8
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:24,850
on.

9
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:28,980
The ceiling: where the next dollar jumps
from twelve cents to twenty-two.

10
00:00:29,670 --> 00:00:33,380
And what four years of empty room costs,
in dollars.

11
00:00:34,160 --> 00:00:36,260
This is education, not tax advice.

12
00:00:36,900 --> 00:00:39,160
The couple is an example, not a real
person.

13
00:00:40,060 --> 00:00:41,060
Start with the floor.

14
00:00:41,580 --> 00:00:45,600
The I R S published the twenty
twenty-seven numbers on November fifth.

15
00:00:45,660 --> 00:00:46,660
Here's the couple.

16
00:00:47,080 --> 00:00:48,080
Both sixty-six.

17
00:00:48,460 --> 00:00:49,350
Retired.

18
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:50,400
No paycheck.

19
00:00:50,760 --> 00:00:52,440
Social Security waits until seventy.

20
00:00:53,100 --> 00:00:56,260
They live on an I R A, ninety thousand
dollars a year.

21
00:00:56,900 --> 00:00:59,960
Every dollar out of that I R A is ordinary
income.

22
00:01:00,440 --> 00:01:04,140
But before the first bracket touches it,
three deductions stack up.

23
00:01:04,620 --> 00:01:09,260
The standard deduction, married filing
jointly: thirty-three thousand two
hundred.

24
00:01:09,760 --> 00:01:14,460
The extra amount for being sixty-five or
older: seventeen hundred each, thirty-four

25
00:01:14,510 --> 00:01:15,470
hundred.

26
00:01:15,520 --> 00:01:19,650
And the senior deduction from the twenty
twenty-five tax law: six thousand dollars

27
00:01:19,700 --> 00:01:21,420
each, twelve thousand.

28
00:01:21,470 --> 00:01:22,560
Add it up.

29
00:01:23,060 --> 00:01:24,500
Forty-eight thousand six hundred dollars.

30
00:01:25,380 --> 00:01:26,380
That's the floor.

31
00:01:26,860 --> 00:01:30,820
The first forty-eight thousand six hundred
they take out is taxed at zero.

32
00:01:31,660 --> 00:01:32,660
How to check yours.

33
00:01:33,180 --> 00:01:35,760
Your filing status and your ages set the
floor.

34
00:01:36,340 --> 00:01:40,650
The three lines are in the I R S revenue
procedure for twenty twenty-seven, number

35
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:42,180
twenty-six dash thirty-five,

36
00:01:42,920 --> 00:01:46,870
and the six thousand starts to shrink
above one hundred fifty thousand of income

37
00:01:46,920 --> 00:01:47,920
for a couple.

38
00:01:49,920 --> 00:01:50,920
Now the ceiling.

39
00:01:51,580 --> 00:01:55,630
Above the floor, the ten percent bracket
runs to twenty-five thousand six hundred

40
00:01:55,680 --> 00:01:56,960
of taxable income.

41
00:01:57,480 --> 00:02:00,680
The twelve percent bracket runs to one
hundred four thousand fifty.

42
00:02:01,340 --> 00:02:05,150
Put the floor under it, and a couple can
pull about one hundred fifty-two thousand

43
00:02:05,200 --> 00:02:09,970
dollars out of an I R A in twenty
twenty-seven before a single dollar is
taxed

44
00:02:10,020 --> 00:02:11,020
at twenty-two.

45
00:02:11,320 --> 00:02:12,270
Run it.

46
00:02:12,320 --> 00:02:14,280
One hundred fifty thousand dollars out.

47
00:02:14,760 --> 00:02:18,500
Minus the floor, that's one hundred one
thousand four hundred taxable.

48
00:02:19,220 --> 00:02:23,730
Ten percent on the first twenty-five
thousand six hundred: two thousand five
hundred

49
00:02:23,780 --> 00:02:24,180
sixty.

50
00:02:24,230 --> 00:02:27,580
Twelve percent on the rest: nine thousand
ninety-six.

51
00:02:28,120 --> 00:02:29,120
Total tax…

52
00:02:29,360 --> 00:02:30,950
eleven thousand six hundred fifty-six
dollars.

53
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:34,220
On one hundred fifty thousand.

54
00:02:34,400 --> 00:02:35,400
Under eight percent.

55
00:02:35,940 --> 00:02:37,280
So here's the couple again.

56
00:02:37,840 --> 00:02:39,280
They take ninety thousand.

57
00:02:39,500 --> 00:02:41,760
Their tax is four thousand four hundred
fifty-six.

58
00:02:42,620 --> 00:02:46,200
And sixty thousand dollars of
twelve-percent room sits empty.

59
00:02:46,780 --> 00:02:48,240
That room doesn't carry over.

60
00:02:48,780 --> 00:02:50,760
On January first, it's gone.

61
00:02:52,320 --> 00:02:54,240
Why does empty room cost anything?

62
00:02:54,840 --> 00:02:56,320
Because of what happens at seventy.

63
00:02:57,020 --> 00:03:01,300
Social Security starts, and up to
eighty-five percent of it counts as
income.

64
00:03:01,940 --> 00:03:06,630
At seventy-five, the required minimum
distributions start, and the I R S sets

65
00:03:06,680 --> 00:03:07,680
the size.

66
00:03:08,320 --> 00:03:12,030
Stack those on top of the withdrawals they
still need, and the same couple is

67
00:03:12,080 --> 00:03:15,060
living in the twenty-two percent bracket,
maybe twenty-four.

68
00:03:15,680 --> 00:03:19,180
The dollars they didn't take at twelve
come out later at twenty-two.

69
00:03:19,840 --> 00:03:21,080
Run the four years.

70
00:03:21,900 --> 00:03:25,420
Twenty twenty-seven through twenty thirty,
sixty-six to sixty-nine.

71
00:03:25,470 --> 00:03:30,730
Sixty thousand dollars of room a year,
four years: two hundred forty thousand

72
00:03:30,780 --> 00:03:31,780
dollars.

73
00:03:32,200 --> 00:03:33,980
Taxed at twelve percent as they go:

74
00:03:34,860 --> 00:03:35,940
twenty-eight thousand eight hundred.

75
00:03:36,620 --> 00:03:40,890
The same two hundred forty thousand taxed
later at twenty-two: fifty-two thousand

76
00:03:40,940 --> 00:03:42,040
eight hundred.

77
00:03:42,660 --> 00:03:43,660
The difference…

78
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:45,160
twenty-four thousand dollars.

79
00:03:46,020 --> 00:03:48,300
More than three months of what they live
on.

80
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:53,160
If the later rate is twenty-four, the gap
is twenty-eight thousand eight hundred.

81
00:03:53,980 --> 00:03:56,560
What filling the room looks like is a Roth
conversion.

82
00:03:57,460 --> 00:04:02,430
Money moved from the I R A to a Roth, the
tax paid now at twelve, and after a

83
00:04:02,480 --> 00:04:04,780
five-year wait, nothing owed on it again.

84
00:04:05,460 --> 00:04:10,170
Whether that's right for you is a question
for a fee-only fiduciary or a C P A

85
00:04:10,220 --> 00:04:14,330
with your return in front of them, because
the answer depends on your state, your

86
00:04:14,380 --> 00:04:16,720
Social Security, and what you'll need
later.

87
00:04:17,560 --> 00:04:18,560
Now the fine print.

88
00:04:18,960 --> 00:04:19,960
Three lines.

89
00:04:20,500 --> 00:04:21,290
One.

90
00:04:21,340 --> 00:04:23,520
The far side of the ceiling isn't
twenty-two.

91
00:04:24,260 --> 00:04:28,830
The six thousand senior deduction shrinks
by six cents for every dollar of income

92
00:04:28,880 --> 00:04:31,720
over one hundred fifty thousand, for each
spouse.

93
00:04:32,360 --> 00:04:36,880
So past one fifty-two, the next dollar
costs about twenty-four and a half cents.

94
00:04:37,520 --> 00:04:39,380
The cliff is steeper than the chart.

95
00:04:39,960 --> 00:04:40,890
Two.

96
00:04:40,940 --> 00:04:42,620
The six thousand is temporary.

97
00:04:43,070 --> 00:04:45,180
The law runs it through twenty
twenty-eight.

98
00:04:45,880 --> 00:04:50,480
If it ends on schedule, years three and
four have twelve thousand less room each.

99
00:04:51,180 --> 00:04:55,600
Cautious case: about two hundred thousand
across the four years, and twenty thousand

100
00:04:55,650 --> 00:04:57,440
dollars saved instead of twenty-four.

101
00:04:58,180 --> 00:04:59,180
Three.

102
00:04:59,920 --> 00:05:01,280
Medicare looks back two years.

103
00:05:01,840 --> 00:05:05,890
The income on your twenty twenty-seven
return sets your twenty twenty-nine Part

104
00:05:05,940 --> 00:05:06,710
B premium.

105
00:05:06,760 --> 00:05:12,140
For twenty twenty-six the first surcharge
line for a couple is two hundred eighteen

106
00:05:12,190 --> 00:05:14,860
thousand, and it's indexed, so it won't be
lower.

107
00:05:15,420 --> 00:05:17,660
The twelve-percent ceiling stays under it.

108
00:05:18,040 --> 00:05:22,670
Fill past the ceiling and you can trip the
surcharge, two years later, with no

109
00:05:22,720 --> 00:05:24,140
letter until it does.

110
00:05:24,740 --> 00:05:25,740
How to check yours.

111
00:05:26,420 --> 00:05:27,520
Pull last year's return.

112
00:05:28,140 --> 00:05:31,580
Find taxable income, line fifteen on
recent forms.

113
00:05:32,140 --> 00:05:34,360
Subtract it from one hundred four thousand
fifty.

114
00:05:34,980 --> 00:05:39,560
That's roughly your room for twenty
twenty-seven, before any Social Security
math,

115
00:05:39,760 --> 00:05:41,060
which is its own episode.

116
00:05:41,560 --> 00:05:42,560
Here's the pattern.

117
00:05:42,920 --> 00:05:47,920
The tax code sets a floor and a ceiling
every year, and resets both every January.

118
00:05:48,200 --> 00:05:52,070
The couple who knows the two numbers pays
twelve cents on the dollar in the years

119
00:05:52,120 --> 00:05:53,120
they can choose.

120
00:05:53,280 --> 00:05:56,770
The couple who doesn't pays twenty-two in
the years they can't.

121
00:05:56,820 --> 00:06:00,850
On a nine hundred thousand dollar I R A,
that's three months of living money,

122
00:06:00,900 --> 00:06:02,240
gone to timing.

123
00:06:03,040 --> 00:06:04,040
One thing this week.

124
00:06:04,500 --> 00:06:05,520
Find line fifteen.

125
00:06:05,960 --> 00:06:06,960
Do the subtraction.

126
00:06:07,360 --> 00:06:10,960
Then ask someone with your whole return in
front of them what the room is worth.

127
00:06:11,520 --> 00:06:13,380
New numbers every Tuesday and Friday.

128
00:06:14,340 --> 00:06:15,820
Subscribe so the next one finds you.

129
00:06:16,960 --> 00:06:19,080
Will your money last as long as you do?

